  132:             {household.name}
  133:           </option>
  134:         ))}
  135:         {households.length === 0 && <option value="">No households</option>}
  136:       </select>
This markup DIFFERS from the page-level selector in three load-bearing ways:
  (a) `aria-label="Household"` (the accessible name; NO <label> wrapper). HouseholdSelector has no
      aria-label prop at all (it uses a wrapping <label> or nothing).
  (b) NO empty option when households exist; a `No households` fallback shown ONLY when empty.
      HouseholdSelector renders `emptyOptionLabel` as a permanent FIRST option whenever truthy, or
      nothing when falsy — it cannot express "fallback only when empty".
  (c) no label wrapper (showLabel={false} on the shared component gives a bare select, which matches
      (c) only).
Guarding test: frontend/src/components/shell/shell.test.tsx:594-606, which depends on the aria-label
(via `getByLabelText("Household")`):
  602:     fireEvent.change(screen.getByLabelText("Household"), { target: { value: "h2" } });

CONSEQUENCE (STOP for call-site 3, per packet G0 clause "if the port becomes impossible inside the
ceiling, STOP that call-site and ship the rest with the STOP committed"):
  A byte-identical port of call-site 3 through HouseholdSelector would require (a) a new
  `aria-label`/accessible-name prop and (b) a conditional empty-fallback option behaviour — i.e. at
  least TWO API changes beyond the single class passthrough the packet allows ("the passthrough prop
  ONLY"; "no other API change"). Dropping either breaks output identity and the shell test above.
  HouseholderSelector is therefore NOT substituted in CatalogToolbar.tsx; that file is left byte-
  untouched. This is a FINDING (F-SG143-2), reported, not force-applied.

--- Reference: already-ported call-sites (MUST stay byte-identical; default prop = unset) ---
ChatPage.tsx:79-86 and PlanningPage.tsx:71-78 both call:
  <HouseholdSelector value={effectiveHousehold} onChange={...} households={households as Household[] | undefined} />
with all presentation props defaulted. Adding an optional `selectClassName` defaulting to undefined
leaves their rendered <select> with NO className attribute — byte-identical. To be proven by test and
by the untouched Chat/Planning suites.

--- The passthrough prop (DECIDED — report per packet) ---
Name: `selectClassName` (optional, `string`, default unset). Rationale: the component can render a
wrapping <label> AND a <select>, so a bare `className` would be ambiguous; `selectClassName` names the
exact element. Applied as `className={selectClassName}` on the <select>. No other prop/API change.

